  <text>
    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="17">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:IICn">II Cn</ref> 17</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="17" corresp="#wit-g-a">[And ne gesece nan man þone] cingc, butan he ne mote beon nanes rihtes wyrðe innan his hundrede.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="17" corresp="#wit-a">And ne gesece nan man þonne cyningce, buton he ne mote beon nanes rihtes wirðe innan his hundrede.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="17" corresp="#wit-q">Nemo regem requirat de iusticia facienda, dum ei rectum offertur in hundreto suo.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#robertson" n="17" corresp="#wit-b">Ne geséce nan man ðonne cyng, buton he ne mote beon nanes rihtes wurðe innan his hundrede.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-b">And no-one shall appeal to the king, unless he fails to obtain justice within his hundred.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann">Und niemand gehe den König an, ausser wenn er innerhalb seiner Hundertschaft [seines] Rechtes nicht theilhaftig werden kann.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">And let no one apply to the king, unless he cannot obtain [his] rights within his hundred.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">G's ‘[And ne gesece nan man þone]’ is written over an erasure in the 16th century. Liebermann compares III Eg 2.</note>
      </div>
    </body>
